            <ArticleTitle>Rotavirus vaccination can be performed without viral dissemination in the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/infdis/jix590</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Background" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">This study was conducted to assess transmissibility of the vaccine strains after RV vaccination in a neonatal intensive care unit (NICU).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Rotateq (RV5) or Rotarix (RV1) was administered to infants admitted to the NICU. Vaccinated infants (VIs, n=19) and unvaccinated infants (UVIs, n=49) whose beds were located in close proximity to the VIs were enrolled in this study. Fecal shedding of vaccine viruses and spreading of the vaccine strains within the NICU were examined using real-time reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Shedding of the vaccine strain was detected in all 19 VIs. RV5 vaccine virus shedding started 1 day after the first vaccination and persisted for 8 days after the 1 st vaccination, and viral shedding terminated by day 5 after the 2 nd RV5 vaccination. The kinetics of RV1 vaccine virus shedding differed among VIs. Duration of the RV1 virus shedding was longer after 1 st vaccination than 2 nd vaccination. In contrast to the VIs, no vaccine virus genomes were detected in any of the stool samples collected from the 49 UVIs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusions" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">This study is direct evidence of no transmission of vaccine strain RV between vaccinated and close proximity unvaccinated babies within a NICU.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
